{
  "slug": "bubble-sort",
  "title": "Bubble Sort",
  "summary": "Compare neighbours and swap inverted pairs until no swaps remain.",
  "usage": "How it works\nEach pass moves the largest remaining value to the end. The suffix is final after each pass. Use for learning adjacent comparisons, not large datasets. The early-exit flag avoids extra passes on sorted input.\n\nProperties and prerequisites\nStable: yes (equal neighbours are not swapped). In-place: yes.\n\nComplexity\nTime: O(n\u00b2) average/worst; O(n) best. Space: O(1).\n\nExample\nInput: [5, 2, 4, 1, 3]. Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].\n\nTry it\nUse Play/Pause, Previous/Next, the timeline, and playback speed. Answer the prediction checkpoints to continue; feedback reveals the expected answer. Python is the line-synchronized walkthrough; Java is an equivalent complete implementation. Sorting functions accept empty arrays, duplicates, negative values, and already sorted inputs. Java methods belong inside a class; Python functions can be called directly.",
  "category": "Sorting",
  "difficulty": "EASY",
  "visualizer": "ARRAY",
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n\u00b2) average/worst; O(n) best",
    "space": "O(1)"
  },
  "pythonSource": "def bubble_sort(a):\n    for end in range(len(a) - 1, 0, -1):\n        swapped = False  # Begin a pass over the unsorted prefix.\n        for j in range(end):\n            if a[j] > a[j + 1]:  # Compare adjacent values.\n                a[j], a[j + 1] = a[j + 1], a[j]  # Swap out-of-order neighbours.\n                swapped = True\n        if not swapped:  # A pass without swaps proves the array is sorted.\n            break\n    return a\n",
  "javaSource": "static void bubbleSort(int[] a) {\n    for (int end = a.length - 1; end > 0; end--) {\n        boolean swapped = false;\n        for (int j = 0; j < end; j++) {\n            if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) {\n                int t = a[j]; a[j] = a[j + 1]; a[j + 1] = t;\n                swapped = true;\n            }\n        }\n        if (!swapped) break;\n    }\n}",
